ELB Types
AWS Elastic Load Balancing (ELB) distributes incoming application traffic across multiple targets, such as EC2 instances, containers, and IP addresses, across one or more Availability Zones.

1. Application Load Balancer (ALB)
- Works at Layer 7 (Application Layer).
- Supports HTTP and HTTPS protocols.
- Supports path-based routing and host-based routing.
- Commonly used for web applications and microservices.

Real-time example:

Suppose an application has two services:
- /payment → Payment EC2 instances.
- /orders → Order EC2 instances.
ALB routes requests based on URL paths.

2. Network Load Balancer (NLB)
- Works at Layer 4 (Transport Layer).
- Supports TCP, UDP and TLS protocols.
- Handles millions of requests per second.
- Provides ultra-low latency.
- Supports static IP addresses.
Real-time example:

For an online gaming application that requires high performance and low latency, we can use NLB.

3. Gateway Load Balancer (GWLB)
- Works at Layer 3 (Network Layer).
- Used to distribute traffic across virtual network appliances.
- Supports security inspection and traffic monitoring.
- Uses the GENEVE protocol.
Real-time example:

When an organization wants to distribute network traffic across multiple firewall appliances, it can use GWLB.

4. Classic Load Balancer (CLB)
- Older generation of AWS Load Balancer.
- Supports Layer 4 and Layer 7.
- Provides basic load balancing.
- Supports EC2 instances.
- Generally recommended to migrate to ALB or NLB for modern applications.
Real-time example:

