How Load Balancers Distribute Traffic across targets
Load balancers act as the "traffic cops" sitting in front of your servers, routing client requests across multiple backend targets (servers, containers, or virtual machines) to ensure high availability, reliability, and optimal performance.

Core Mechanics of Traffic Distribution
At a high level, a load balancer receives incoming traffic from clients, evaluates the request, and forwards it to a healthy target using predefined algorithms and health checks.

Listener Configuration: The load balancer listens on a specific port and protocol (e.g., HTTP on port 80, HTTPS on port 443).

Health Checks: Before routing any traffic, the load balancer continuously pings backend targets to verify they are healthy and responding. If a server fails, traffic is automatically rerouted to remaining healthy targets.

Routing Algorithm: Once a request hits the listener, the load balancer applies a specific algorithm to select the best target for that request.

Common Load Balancing Algorithms
Different algorithms determine how traffic is divided among targets. Here are the most widely used methods:

Round Robin:

How it works: Distributes requests sequentially across the list of servers. The first request goes to Server A, the second to Server B, the third to Server C, and then it loops back to Server A.

Best used when: Servers have roughly equal specifications and workloads are similar.

Weighted Round Robin:

How it works: Similar to standard round robin, but administrators assign a "weight" to each server based on its capacity. A server with a weight of 3 will receive three times as many requests as a server with a weight of 1.

Best used when: Backend servers have varying processing power or hardware specs.

Least Connections:

How it works: Directs new requests to the server that currently has the fewest active connections.

Best used when: Applications involve long-lived sessions (e.g., chat applications or database connections) where server processing times can vary significantly.

Weighted Least Connections:

How it works: Combines the least connections method with server weights, routing traffic based on active connections relative to each server's assigned capacity.

IP Hash (Source IP):

How it works: Generates a unique hash key based on the client's IP address and routes the client to the same server every time.

Best used when: Session persistence is required (e.g., shopping cart data stored locally on a server rather than in a shared database).
An organization running an older application on EC2 can continue using CLB.

Target Groups & Health Checks — configuration 
Target Groups (Where Traffic Goes)
A logical pool of backend servers (VMs, containers, IP addresses) that receive load-balanced traffic.
Target Type: Identifies targets by Instance ID, IP address, or Lambda function.
Protocol & Port: The port/protocol (e.g., HTTP:8080) the load balancer uses to forward requests to the target.
Sticky Sessions: Binds a user's session to one specific target for continuity.
Deregistration Delay: Pauses before removing a target so active requests can finish safely (connection draining).
Health Checks (Traffic Safety Pings)
Automated probes that verify whether a backend target is alive before sending it traffic.
Endpoint & Protocol: The path pinged to test health (e.g., GET /health).
Interval & Timeout: How often to check (e.g., every 15s) and how long to wait for a response (e.g., 5s).
Thresholds: The number of consecutive passes (e.g., 2) or fails (e.g., 3) needed to mark a server Healthy or Unhealthy.
Success Codes: Acceptable HTTP responses indicating health (e.g., 200 OK).

SSL/TLS Termination at ELB — certificate management 
How It Works
Decrypt at Load Balancer: Incoming encrypted HTTPS traffic is decrypted by the load balancer using an SSL certificate.
Forward to Backends: Traffic is sent to internal targets either as plain HTTP (offloading server CPU load) or re-encrypted HTTPS (end-to-end security).
Certificate Management
AWS Certificate Manager (ACM): Provision free SSL certificates that auto-renew, eliminating downtime from expired certs.
External Uploads (IAM): Import third-party certificates, requiring manual tracking and manual renewal.
SNI (Server Name Indication): Bind multiple SSL certs to a single load balancer port to serve different domain names (app.com, api.com).
Core Security Controls
TLS Policy: Force modern encryption standards (e.g., TLS 1.2 or 1.3 only) to block vulnerable old protocols.
End-to-End Encryption: Re-encrypt traffic between the load balancer and backends to meet strict compliance rules (PCI-DSS, HIPAA).

Cross-Zone Load Balancing behavior 
How It Works
Disabled: Each load balancer node only sends traffic to targets in its own Availability Zone (AZ).
Enabled: Load balancer nodes share traffic across all healthy targets in all AZs equally.
Real-World Example
Imagine you have two Availability Zones (AZ-A and AZ-B) with one server in each zone. Due to user behavior, 90% of your incoming traffic hits AZ-A, and only 10% hits AZ-B.
Without Cross-Zone (Disabled):
The load balancer in AZ-A tries to split its 90% traffic across its local server. Result: Your AZ-A server crashes from overload, while your AZ-B server sits mostly idle.
With Cross-Zone (Enabled):
The load balancer pools both servers together. Regardless of where the traffic enters, it routes 45% of the total traffic to the server in AZ-A and 45% to the server in AZ-B. Result: Balanced workload and zero downtime.

      High Availability with ELB across multiple AZs 
Core Architecture
Multi-Node Setup: AWS automatically provisions a load balancer node in each enabled Availability Zone (AZ).
DNS Integration: Client DNS queries return IP addresses for nodes across all active zones. If an entire AZ fails, DNS automatically routes traffic away from it.
Failover Mechanism
Normal State: Traffic is split across load balancer nodes and backend targets in all active zones.
AZ Outage: If AZ-A dies, health checks immediately mark its targets as unhealthy.
Instant Recovery: Traffic is automatically rerouted to surviving targets in AZ-B (zero downtime for users).
Best Practices for HA
Use 2–3 AZs: Spread infrastructure across multiple zones to absorb extra traffic if one zone fails.
Pair with Auto Scaling Groups (ASGs): Ensure backend targets are distributed evenly across your chosen AZs.
Keep Apps Stateless: Store user sessions in external databases (like Redis or RDS) so users don't lose data if traffic shifts to a different zone.

